def B(i): return re.sub(r'^“[^”]*”\s*:?\s*-?\s*','',O(i),count=1)
def ed(s,*pairs):
    for a,b in pairs: s=sub(s,a,b)
    return s
s0=h.index('<h2>Summary</h2>\n<p>'); s1=h.index('</p>',s0)
h=h[:s0]+'<h2>Summary</h2>\n<p>Puffing along the shore with his mantle growing around him, Mr. Spitzer cannot tell whether he is the conductor lifting his baton, the lamp-lighter, or the oarsman striking light from the waves of darkness. He is the doctor who has lost his patient, the lawyer who has lost his client, lawyer to a starfish, a dead coachman, and the moon in the cloud, and the old postman knocking at every door. He fears being right more than being wrong, removes the stones from a road and puts one back lest he change rivers in their course, and trusts the past more than the future. He signs illegal documents as if they were legal and stamps wills with the wrong seals, once with the seal of the Baltimore and Ohio Railroad, while Mr. Mouse gnaws his lawsuits and eats his chords. Respecting other people’s mirages because he is a mirage himself, he meets a lady whose twirling zodiac umbrella moves time, an old woman who thinks she is a bird cage, deaf music critics who hear every error, two pianists who play in perfect time only when a thousand miles apart, and an old woman who believed she was a reindeer and was found with a golden arrow through her breast. He lives by mourning the death of the deathless one, and he concludes that the dream of life, like that of death, must ever contain the unrealized, else all would be complete.</p>'+h[s1+4:]
G=[('Reading closely',['“A flaw in the creation had permitted this creation.” How can a flaw be the reason anything exists?','“He had made of a dead kite-flyer his premise, of a muted bell-ringer his theory, of an old lamp-lighter his love.” What is Mr. Spitzer’s philosophy?','“His effort to go straight had made him crooked.” Why does every straight road turn circular for him?']),
('Characters',['Mr. Mouse gnaws Mr. Spitzer’s lawsuits and symphonies, and yet “the greatest impresario was the mouse.” Is the mouse his enemy or his collaborator?','Mr. Spitzer “would remove every stone from a road” and then put a stone back. What does his caution protect?','The old woman who believed she was a reindeer “was found in bed with a golden arrow driven through her breast.” What does her fate say about illusions that come true?']),
('Connections',['In Chapter 50 Mr. Spitzer feared “that one should be one.” Here he “might have dropped dead if he had discovered… that his brother lived.” Why is success what he fears?','The opium lady travels the world by night with a black coachman whose face she has never seen. Where have the coachman and the night journeys appeared before?','Mr. Spitzer stamps wills with the seal of the Baltimore and Ohio Railroad and imagines a world of all trains that have passed away. How do trains and stations carry the dead through the novel?']),
('Reflection',['“In most lives, three minutes of happiness seemed perhaps all there might be.” Is that too little, or enough?','“He had learned to respect other people’s mirages.” Should one?','“For the dream of life, even like that of death, must ever contain within it an element of the unrealized.” Is what remains unrealized a loss?'])]
rp('<h2>Questions and observations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li></li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n','<h2>Questions and observations</h2>\n'+'\n'.join('<h3>%s</h3>\n<ul>\n%s\n</ul>'%(t,'\n'.join('<li>'+q+'</li>' for q in qs)) for t,qs in G)+'\n')
