rp("the very foundations of Mr. Spitzer's world – law, art, and history.","the very foundations of Mr. Spitzer's world: law, art, and history.")
rp('<h3>Illusion vs. Reality</h3>','<h3>Illusion and Reality</h3>')
rp("<li>It's possible to interpret Mr. Mouse","<li>It is possible to interpret Mr. Mouse")
E=[]
def add(k,b,kids=()): E.append((k,b,list(kids)))
add('the hounds of Nimrod barking through clouds','“He was a mighty hunter before the LORD” (Genesis 10:9). A nimrod is now a skillful hunter, or, in later American slang, an inept person. The huntsman of the stars is Orion; see the '+C(49)+'.'+see(BI,AS))
add('like the sweepings of the arms of the great semaphores','A semaphore is an apparatus that sends visual signals over distance with flags or mechanical arms, used at sea and on railways; Claude Chappe’s semaphore towers carried messages across France from 1794. Mr. Spitzer’s consciousness is fragmented and overwhelmed by countless signals and impressions, some clear, some obscure, and the semaphores are that confusing signaling around and within him, which he tries to interpret amid the fog and chaos of his mind and life.')
add('this old postman going his rounds through all inclemencies','“Neither snow nor rain nor heat nor gloom of night stays these couriers from the swift completion of their appointed rounds,” carved on the New York General Post Office in 1914, adapts Herodotus on the Persian mounted couriers (Histories 8.98).')
add('lawyer for a dead coachman','See The Black Coachman, and the fringe-topped surrey in the '+C(49)+'.'+see(BC))
add('the withering of the flower before the blooming of the flower','Effect before cause, like the White Queen’s messenger punished before the crime; see the '+C(48)+'.'+see(AL))
add('ferried by a Charon of sea shells','Charon is the ferryman of Hades in Greek mythology; he returns later in the chapter, reaching “with his oar upon the darkening waters.” See Charon in the '+C(46)+'.'+see(GM))
add('he would remove every stone from a road for fear the next wayfarer might be hurt',O(10)+see(HI))
add('he might change rivers in their course','Mr. Spitzer’s fear is the butterfly effect of chaos theory: small, subtle causes yielding large, unpredictable consequences in a complex, interwoven reality. Chaos theory studies systems, like weather or ecosystems, so sensitive to their starting conditions that a tiny change can lead to vastly different outcomes, though underlying patterns, feedback loops, and self-organization remain (Wikipedia). The meteorologist Edward Lorenz named the effect in a 1972 talk, “Predictability: Does the Flap of a Butterfly’s Wings in Brazil Set Off a Tornado in Texas?” Ray Bradbury had already imagined it in “A Sound of Thunder” (1952), where a time traveler steps on a butterfly and changes history. “For the chaos had its pattern.”'+see(TP,OC))
add('the prisms of Mr. Chandelier','See Mr. Chandelier.'+see(MCH))
add('a restless suffrage rider','Cousin Hannah, “who had always mistaken him for another.”'+see(CH))
add('Was life a legal fiction','A legal fiction is something the law assumes to be true for its purposes though it may not be literally true. Mr. Spitzer asks whether life itself is such a fiction, taken on faith but unprovable either way. The imaginary house with its ivory porches no waves should ever reach is a fragile, beautiful, perhaps illusory construct, complete-seeming but isolated and fading, “its lights already lost.” What seems real and substantial may be a delicate illusion that cannot withstand change and mortality.'+see(GI))
add('scattering clouds of ink like the ink of the squid','Squid and octopus release ink in blobs held together by mucus, pseudomorphs, that keep roughly the shape of the animal and draw a predator’s attack while the animal escapes.')
add('scattering sand over his paper from a ram’s horn','Before blotting paper came into wide use in the nineteenth century, writers dried wet ink by sprinkling sand from a pounce pot or sander.')
add('great windhovers sleeping on the wing','The windhover is the kestrel, which hangs on the wind; “I caught this morning morning’s minion, kingdom of daylight’s dauphin, dapple-dawn-drawn Falcon” (Gerard Manley Hopkins, “The Windhover,” written 1877, published 1918). See Hopkins in the '+C(47)+'.')
add('some old lazar with his tinkling bell','Lepers in the Middle Ages had to warn of their approach with a bell or clapper; lazar comes from the beggar Lazarus, “full of sores” (Luke 16:20).'+see(BI))
add('a resurrecting groundhog who had not noticed the presence of his shadow','By folk belief, if the groundhog sees his shadow on February 2 there will be six more weeks of winter, and if he does not, an early spring; the Punxsutawney, Pennsylvania, Groundhog Day began in 1887.')
add('his circular eye which seemed to turn in his forehead',O(14)+' In Hindu tradition it is the ajna chakra between the brows, and the third eye of Shiva burns with the fire of destruction.'+see(HI))
add('He listened with the cherub’s ear and heard an elfin horn','“O sweet and far from cliff and scar / The horns of Elfland faintly blowing!” (Alfred Tennyson, “The splendour falls,” The Princess, 1850).')
add('a dolphin’s skin should have been his clothing','See Byron’s dying dolphin in the '+C(50)+'.'+see(RP))
add('the coal packs in the Milky Way','The Coalsack is a dark nebula in the Southern Cross, a cloud of dust that blots out the stars behind it; there are smaller dark lanes all along the Milky Way.'+see(AS))
add('Did not the live coal burn upon his lips','“Then flew one of the seraphims unto me, having a live coal in his hand… And he laid it upon my mouth, and said, Lo, this hath touched thy lips; and thine iniquity is taken away” (Isaiah 6:6-7).'+see(BI))
add('balls of ethereal gold which seem to hang around the masts','St. Elmo’s fire; see corpo santo in the '+C(47)+'.')
add('the glassy cricket singing in the chimney fire','Charles Dickens’s The Cricket on the Hearth (1845) is a fairy tale of a household guarded by the cricket chirping by the fire.')
add('Man might be the finest musician','A person may have great gifts, but without connection, engagement, or the response of others those gifts fall silent, like an instrument no one plays, “an empty wooden box like the coffin of music strung with a few cat-gut strings.” Life gains meaning through being expressed, “even if played upon only by the fingers of the wind,” and the line laments the silence after death, part of the novel’s interplay of presence and absence, sound and silence.'+see(MI))
add('He had made of a dead kite-flyer his premise','These may refer to Ben Franklin, Boethius, and Diogenes. The muted bell-ringer may also be Quasimodo, the deaf bell-ringer of Victor Hugo’s The Hunchback of Notre-Dame (1831).',
 ['Benjamin Franklin (1706-1790) was an American polymath: printer, writer, scientist, inventor, statesman, and diplomat, one of the Founding Fathers, a drafter and signer of the Declaration of Independence, and the first postmaster general. His kite experiment of 1752 showed that lightning is electricity, and Anne Robert Jacques Turgot said of him, “Eripuit fulmen cœlo, mox sceptra tyrannis” (“He snatched lightning from the sky and the scepter from tyrants”). At some points in his life he owned slaves and ran “for sale” ads for slaves in his newspaper, but by the late 1750s he began arguing against slavery, became an active abolitionist, and promoted the education and integration of African Americans. He invented the lightning rod, bifocals, the glass harmonica, and the Franklin stove.'+W,
  'The Ben Franklin effect is the finding that people like someone more after doing him a favor, explained by cognitive dissonance. Franklin quoted an “old maxim” in his autobiography: “He that has once done you a kindness will be more ready to do you another, than he whom you yourself have obliged,” and told how he won over a rival legislator by borrowing a rare book from him.'+W,
  'Boethius (about 477-524) was a Roman senator, consul, and philosopher who sought to translate Plato and Aristotle into Latin; largely through his translations the works of Aristotle survived. His works on arithmetic, geometry, and music, with astronomy, formed the quadrivium, the core of the medieval liberal arts, in which music was the mathematical study of harmony, ratio, and proportion rather than performance. Imprisoned by Theodoric for defending a fellow consul against charges of conspiracy, he wrote On the Consolation of Philosophy, a treatise on fortune and death, before he was tortured and executed in 524.'+W+see(AR),
  'Diogenes the Cynic (about 412-323 BCE) rejected wealth and convention, lived in a large jar, called himself “a citizen of the world,” and carried a lit lantern in daylight, saying he was looking for a man (Diogenes Laertius 6.41). He is the old lamp-lighter.'+W+see(LC)])
add('if reality had been a horse',O(16)+' The parable is told in the Buddhist Udana (6.4) and in John Godfrey Saxe’s poem “The Blind Men and the Elephant” (1872).'+see(BU,HC))
add('Had he given birth to his dead twin','See Peron Spitzer.'+see(PE,TW))
add('all riding for the Holy Grail','See the Holy Grail in the '+C(47)+'.')
add('Perhaps he was twelve cloaks','Mr. Spitzer cannot define himself; he shifts between roles and images. Twelve cloaks suggest concealment, disguise, and many layers of identity, the number perhaps recalling the twelve apostles or the twelve signs of the zodiac. The islands of flowers in the flood are beauty and fragility amid chaos, isolated havens like the pockets of meaning he makes in a world of flux. The stalled sail is stagnation, the sail breaking into flame sudden and violent change. The ram’s horn, the shofar, is a call to awakening, remembrance, and the divine, and “metamorphosed” makes him not a fixed entity but a being always changing.'+see(JU))
add('a man of many gates, gates of ivory, gates of gold','“There are two gates through which these unsubstantial fancies proceed; the one is of horn, and the other ivory. Those that come through the gate of ivory are fatuous, but those from the gate of horn mean something to those that see them” (Homer, Odyssey 19, trans. Samuel Butler, 1900).'+see(HO,DI))
add('he trusted the past more than he trusted the future','Mr. Spitzer’s uncertainty about identity, reality, and knowledge fits Popper’s view that knowledge is provisional: “Perhaps all definitions failed where each was each and each was the other.” He is “a man of many parts, many branches, a man of many faded horizons,” an identity that defies simple categorization, as for Popper knowledge is never complete. His trust of the past comes from uncertainty about the present and future rather than from belief in the past’s truth, and Popper would caution against blindly accepting any authority, including the authority of the past. He knows he may be “the decoy and not the man,” “deceived by many facades,” his perceptions unreliable, and he has found “the impossibility of committing suicide, for he had died merely by living, by continuing from moment to moment.” In the book, Open Society, Popper warned of the dangers of idealizing the past and assuming there is some perfect past that society can return to and make everything better. For example, the idea that the 1950s were perfect and that society could just go back to that time. Or the Dark Enlightenment bros wanting to go back even further to The Gilded Age and its perceived “perfection”.',
 ['Sir Karl Popper (1902-1994) was an Austrian-British philosopher of science who rejected the classical inductivist view of the scientific method in favor of empirical falsification: a theory in the empirical sciences can never be proven, but it can be falsified, and should be scrutinized with decisive experiments. Scientific progress is conjecture and refutation, bold guesses tested by attempts to disprove them; objective knowledge exists, but access to it is always provisional; and no number of confirming instances can make an induction certain. He defended liberal democracy and the open society, which embraces critical thinking, tolerance, and the willingness to challenge established ideas, and opposed totalitarianism, nationalism, and fascism (The Open Society and Its Enemies, 1945).'+W,
  'Historicism is the belief that history unfolds according to fixed laws toward a predetermined end. Popper argued that historicists mistake historical trends for laws; that since the growth of knowledge shapes history and future discoveries cannot be predicted, the future course of history cannot be predicted; that history is unique and non-repetitive; and that historicism is politically dangerous, underpinning totalitarian ideologies and utopian social engineering that disregard individual freedom (The Poverty of Historicism, 1957).'+W])
add('Two tenants wrangled in an empty house','Mr. Spitzer is in constant conflict with himself: the empty house is the body, and the wrangling tenants, body and soul, suggest no fixed self or objective truth about who he is. He would sue for “a false spring,” an illusion not to be trusted, and he is caught “between two worlds, between two lives, between two deaths.” Gorgias’s three claims run through the passage: he cannot know himself; the drifting mirrors show that what could be known could not be communicated; he mixes up greetings and farewells. “He had already gone, and yet he remained,” the paradox of existence in a world where nothing truly exists, and his confusing his conscience with his consciousness shows a world without objective value, where accuracy becomes irrelevant.',
 ['Gorgias (about 483-376 BCE) was a Greek sophist and rhetorician from Leontinoi in Sicily. W. K. C. Guthrie writes that like other sophists he traveled from city to city, giving public exhibitions at Olympia and Delphi and charging fees for his instruction, and that “a special feature of his displays was to ask miscellaneous questions from the audience and give impromptu replies” (A History of Greek Philosophy, vol. 3, 1969). He has been called “Gorgias the Nihilist,” though how well the epithet fits is disputed, and he carried rhetoric from Sicily to Athens.'+W,
  'In his On Non-Existence, as Sextus Empiricus reports it (Against the Logicians 1.65-87), Gorgias argued that nothing exists; that if something did exist, it could not be known; and that if something could be known, it could not be communicated.'])
add('the great planetary wheel turning like a ferris wheel','George Ferris built the first Ferris wheel for the Chicago World’s Fair of 1893. Fortune’s wheel is older: in Boethius, Fortune says, “I turn the wheel that spins. I delight to see the high come down and the low ascend” (The Consolation of Philosophy 2.2, trans. W. V. Cooper, 1902).'+see(GP))
add('A flaw in the creation had permitted this creation','In the Kabbalah of Isaac Luria (sixteenth century), the vessels that held God’s light shattered at the creation, the shevirat ha-kelim, the breaking of the vessels, so that the world was made through a break. Mr. Spitzer’s errors “had never evolved from an original sin,” Augustine’s doctrine of the sin inherited from Adam.'+see(JU,AU))
add('a golden-footed compass point moving through a cloud','“He took the golden compasses, prepared / In God’s eternal store, to circumscribe / This universe” (John Milton, Paradise Lost 7.225-227), the figure William Blake drew in The Ancient of Days (1794).')
add('a bridge made of the wings of birds','In the Chinese legend of the Cowherd and the Weaver Girl, the lovers are parted by the Milky Way and may meet once a year, on the seventh night of the seventh month, on a bridge made of magpies’ wings.')
add('He had learned to respect other people’s mirages','Because he exists only as a mirage himself, Mr. Spitzer has learned to respect the illusions of others, accepting that each person constructs a reality that is deeply personal and meaningful. Yet he dreams of “that blind Eros” for whom there is no time and no face of love, a love beyond the individual and beyond life and death, relying on no recognition of subject or object. The only light he sees by shines “through his finger-tips lighting clouds and fogs,” insight from an intuitive, almost mystical source, turning “old trees to trees of fire… as if a star had passed where there was no star.” The shore beyond is “bordered by no sea,” inviting and forbidding, a place of catastrophe, “whether it was death or love. And perhaps they could not be divided.”',
 [O(17)+' “Love looks not with the eyes, but with the mind; / And therefore is wing’d Cupid painted blind” (A Midsummer Night’s Dream 1.1).'+see(GM,SH)])
add('Never should he see his brother’s face','Mr. Spitzer can see his brother only “through all the mazed images of the dead, through all the differences.” He lives through death no more miraculously than “some crazy old gambler who had thrown his last dice,” and he tries “to go legal,” as biased as “a left-handed portrait painter in a world of right-handed portrait painters.” “His effort to go straight had made him crooked,” and his way is “finally circular.” He practices only “in the courts which were paved with the moonstones of lost cases under a firmament of lost stars,” and he prefers “the seething silence to the sound, preferring mystery to exposure, preferring a cloak to nakedness.”'+see(PE,GP))
add('trying to practice law where there was no law and no lawyer','The rule of law holds that everyone, the government included, is accountable to laws that are publicly known, equally enforced, and independently judged: “no one is above the law.” Mr. Spitzer practices where there is no law at all.'+see(GI))
add('old lawsuits for heart’s balm','Heart balm suits, for breach of promise to marry or alienation of affections, let a jilted or betrayed lover sue for damages; Indiana abolished them in 1935, and many states followed.')
add('perhaps had walked off the roof like his poor brother','Here Peron walked off the roof of the building and not through the window as earlier, which brings to mind the 1929 stock market crash and the stories of investors and brokers jumping from buildings. The wave of such suicides is largely an urban myth, amplified by sensational newspapers and the dark humor of the time; John Kenneth Galbraith called it a legend (The Great Crash, 1929, 1955). There were suicides: a brokerage firm clerk and a member of the New York Mercantile Exchange jumped to their deaths in the financial district in the weeks after the crash, others died by gas, gun, or poison, and the suicide rate rose as the Depression deepened, from 17.0 per 100,000 people in 1929 to 21.3 in 1932. Winston Churchill, in New York at the time, wrote of a man who fell from a building near his hotel (Wikipedia). Although Miss MacIntosh pays a visit to Wall Street and invests “imaginary money,” it is not mentioned if Peron or Joachim invested in Wall Street.'+see(SU,PE))
add('that darkened glass through which he might view his own eclipse','“For now we see through a glass, darkly; but then face to face” (1 Corinthians 13:12). Eclipses were watched through smoked or darkened glass.'+see(BI))
add('perhaps a great President’s seal to indicate a buried nation',O(19),
 [O(20),O(21)+' He was shot in July 1881 and died that September.',O(22)+see(CW),O(23)+' '+O(24),O(25),'A scratch sheet is a racing sheet listing the day’s entries and the horses scratched from them.',O(27),
  'A hook and ladder company is a fire company whose truck carries ladders and hooks; a Hook and Ladder Society would be a firemen’s benevolent society.',O(29)])
add('drank the waters of nepenthe','Nepenthe is a drug described in Homer’s Odyssey as banishing grief: Helen drugs the wine “with an herb that banishes all care, sorrow, and ill humour” (Odyssey 4, trans. Samuel Butler, 1900). A stirrup cup is a last drink taken in the saddle before riding off.'+see(HO))
add('the seal of the Baltimore and Ohio Railroad',O(31)+' Chartered in 1827, it merged into the Chessie System in 1972 and into CSX in 1987, so it has since become extinct, as Mr. Spitzer half expected.')
add('passenger pigeons who were already of another world','See Martha, the last passenger pigeon, in the '+C(49)+'.'+see(MP))
add('some old, dreaming tortoise with the stars upon his back','In Hindu myth Vishnu as the tortoise Kurma bears the world on his back, and for the Lenape and other peoples North America is Turtle Island, the earth on a great turtle’s back.'+see(HI))
add('when he was crossing Beacon Hill','Beacon Hill is a historic neighborhood in Boston, Massachusetts, and the location of the Massachusetts State House; “Beacon Hill” stands for the state government much as Capitol Hill does in Washington. Federal-style rowhouses, narrow streets, and brick sidewalks run through it, and it is one of the more desirable and expensive neighborhoods in Boston.'+W)
add('the crab and the bull and the ram and the goat',ed(O(70),('but Young’s lists only 8 - Cancer','but Young lists only 8: Cancer'))+' The missing four are the lion, the scales, the archer, and the fishes, and Mr. Spitzer asks, “but where was his firefly, and where was his mouse?”'+see(AA,UM))
add('by mental telepathy','J. B. Rhine’s card-guessing experiments at Duke University made telepathy a subject of laboratory study (Extra-Sensory Perception, 1934).')
add('two pianos in the silence','John Cage’s 4′33″ (1952), first played by the pianist David Tudor, is four minutes and thirty-three seconds in which the performer plays nothing, so that the music is the sound of the room.'+see(MC))
add('like Gabriel blowing his horn','The Bible says “the trumpet shall sound, and the dead shall be raised” (1 Corinthians 15:52) but does not name the trumpeter; Gabriel’s horn is later tradition.'+see(BI))
add('His head roared, and his eyes were blurred by visions','This paragraph references two philosophies.',[ed(O(73),('our individual perceptions obscure','individual perceptions obscure')),O(74)+see(EXI)])
add('the squawking of a fire bird','Igor Stravinsky’s ballet The Firebird (1910) is the tale of a magical glowing bird of Russian folklore.'+see(MC))
add('the sky was filled with mock moons','Mock moons, paraselenae, are bright spots on either side of the moon made by ice crystals in high clouds.'+see(AS))
add('had joined a majority','To join the majority is to die, after the Latin abiit ad plures, “he has gone to the many” (Petronius, Satyricon 42).')
add('The copestone was missing like the arch of the sky','“The stone which the builders refused is become the head stone of the corner” (Psalm 118:22).'+see(BI))
add('there was only Mr. Spitzer coming with his knock','There is “nothing dependable.” Everything repeats and doubles before Mr. Spitzer’s eyes, and the boundary between life and death dissolves: he is “already dead or was of two worlds,” perhaps not even “this visitor the dying saw,” and there is “no threshold between world and world.” “We were all transmuted while we lived”: change is inherent to existence, regardless of death. Another world might be “this world which was itself these phantoms and these dreams dreaming themselves,” a form of idealism, and “only one great heartbeat sounding through all of silence” suggests a monistic unity, torn by “the silks of the universe being torn in two and many splittings.”')
add('a mandarin with a long cue','The queue was forced on Han Chinese men by the Manchu Qing rulers in 1645 on pain of death, and Western caricature later made it a racist emblem of the Chinese.'+see(SR))
add('the silks of the universe being torn in two','“And, behold, the veil of the temple was rent in twain from the top to the bottom” (Matthew 27:51).'+see(BI))
add('We were all transmuted while we lived','“We shall not all sleep, but we shall all be changed, In a moment, in the twinkling of an eye” (1 Corinthians 15:51-52).'+see(BI))
add('Seldom did he meet with anyone who did not suffer from some form of complex illusion beyond necessity','Most people Mr. Spitzer meets suffer “some form of complex illusion beyond necessity”: the old woman who believes she is a bird cage, the piano-tuner declaring foreheads “hollow.” Blindness and deafness become metaphors for the limits of perception: the blind portrait painter, the blind war veteran in the galleries of Europe, the deaf music critics who hear every error without hearing a note, judgment resting on something other than the senses. “One great dream informing all dreamers and all dreams” recalls a collective unconscious or universal mind, or Plato’s Form of the Good, the source all strive toward behind the shadows of sense. Happiness is fleeting as a snowflake; “three minutes of happiness seemed perhaps all there might be,” and “the greatest happiness might come through the sorrow of waiting, of postponement.” The passage holds the absurdity of existence and, through Mr. Spitzer’s work for the dead, the responsibility of making sense of their lives.'+see(PL,FJ,DS))
add('tapping every forehead with his tuning fork','“We are the hollow men / We are the stuffed men” (T. S. Eliot, “The Hollow Men,” 1925).')
add('He had once known a blind war veteran',B(78))
add('the most famous music critics were the deaf','Beethoven was nearly deaf when his Ninth Symphony was first played in 1824, and he had to be turned around to see the applause.'+see(MC,DS))
add('had travelled with a black coachman','See The Black Coachman and the opium lady’s night travels in the '+C(33)+'.'+see(CC,BC))
add('she would never be married until she married the emperor of China','The last emperor of China, Puyi, abdicated as a child in 1912.')
add('a golden arrow driven through her breast','Artemis, the huntress, is “of the golden arrows” in Homer, and when Actaeon saw her bathing she turned him into a stag and his own hounds tore him apart (Ovid, Metamorphoses 3).'+see(GM))
add('Most especially she had feared a man in a red coat','English fox hunters ride in red coats, called hunting pink.')
add('an old man who had been cuckolded','The cuckold was said to grow horns, so the antlers on her brow are the cuckold’s horns.')
add('what infinite paradoxes there were in the lidded or the lidless box','The box, lidded or lidless, holds the contradictions of existence: the lidded box closed, limited, secretive; the lidless box open but vulnerable and uncertain. Things contained and hidden, truths revealed and concealed, presence and absence, life and death are intertwined in ways that defy simple resolution, and Mr. Spitzer is caught in these paradoxes as he tries to reconcile his fragmented identity.',
 ['The lidded box may recall Schrödinger’s cat, Erwin Schrödinger’s 1935 thought experiment: a cat sealed in a box with a device that has a 50 percent chance of releasing poison is, by the Copenhagen interpretation of quantum mechanics, both alive and dead until the box is opened and observed. Schrödinger meant it to show the absurdity of applying quantum superposition to everyday objects.'+W+see(TP),
  'Eugene Wigner imagined in 1961 a friend inside a laboratory who sees a measurement while Wigner, outside, must treat friend and laboratory as hovering in a blur of possible states, so that the two seem to occupy distinct realities. In 2019 physicists tested a version with entangled photons and found that two observers could hold irreconcilable records of the same event (Massimiliano Proietti and others, “Experimental test of local observer independence,” Science Advances, 2019). QBism, developed by Christopher Fuchs and others from the early 2000s, holds that each person’s quantum states are personal beliefs built from his own interactions with the world, and the shared reality emerges from those collisions. In perception, a 2022 study found that what is seen at any moment blends with what was seen over the previous fifteen seconds or so (Mauro Manassi and David Whitney, Science Advances, 2022).'+see(TP)])
add('Who knew what was real?',O(12)+' “I see so manifestly that there are no certain indications by which we may clearly distinguish wakefulness from sleep” (René Descartes, Meditations on First Philosophy, Meditation I, 1641, trans. Elizabeth S. Haldane, 1911).'+see(DE),
 ['Mysterianism, named by Owen Flanagan in 1991 and argued by Colin McGinn, holds that the human mind may be too limited to solve the mind-body problem, since the cognitive capabilities of all organisms are limited by biology. William James found this liberating in “Is Life Worth Living?” (1895), where a dog in a laboratory cannot grasp the purposes of the men around it, and wrote, “I believe rather that we stand in much the same relation to the whole of the universe as our canine and feline pets do to the whole of human life” (Pragmatism, 1907, lecture 8).'+see(WJ)])
add('He feared the naked valet who had clothed him','The “naked valet” and the “mad couturier” who clothes him “with the colors of a dream” are outside forces shaping the perception of self and world, as in Plato’s cave, and Thomas Carlyle’s philosophy of clothes in Sartor Resartus (1833-1834) makes the body and the world garments of the spirit. “For the dream of life, even like that of death, must ever contain within it an element of the unrealized, the unrealizable—else all would be complete”: imperfection and the unattainable are part of existence, as existentialist thought holds. Mr. Spitzer fears eviction from the womb, the “citadel” where he heard “the beating of another heart above his heart,” and birth into a world where he will miss the smell of the rose, and the mother who is “a bearded man armored” unsettles fixed ideas of gender. The old jockey who dies of shock when his dream proves real shows the power of the unconscious, as in Freud. “We lived by an imperfect concatenation” echoes the Buddhist teaching of impermanence and the illusory self.'+see(FJ,BU,CL))
T=open(S+'c51.txt').read(); T=re.sub(r'-\n\s*','-',T); T=re.sub(r'\s+',' ',T).replace('’',"'").lower()
pos=lambda k:T.find(k.replace('’',"'").lower())
for k,_,_ in E: assert pos(k)>=0,k
E.sort(key=lambda e:pos(e[0]))
a0=h.index('<h2>Research: Resources</h2>\n<ul>\n')
def li(k,b,kids): return '<li>“%s”: %s'%(k,b)+('\n<ul>\n'+''.join('<li>%s</li>\n'%x for x in kids)+'</ul>\n' if kids else '')+'</li>'
h=h[:a0]+'<h2>Research: Resources</h2>\n<ul>\n'+'\n'.join(li(*e) for e in E)+'\n</ul>\n'
V=[('Amphora','A tall two-handled jar of ancient Greece and Rome.','amphoras?|amphorae'),('Arcana','Secrets; mysteries known only to the initiated.','arcana'),('Copestone','The top stone of a wall or building; the crowning touch.','copestones?'),
('Impresario','The manager of an opera or concert company.','impresarios?'),('Intestate','Having made no will.','intestate'),('Laconic','Using very few words.','laconic'),('Legatee','One who receives a legacy.','legatees?'),
('Lepidopterist','One who studies butterflies and moths.','lepidopterists?'),('Liquescent','Melting or becoming liquid.','liquescent'),('Mock moon','A bright spot beside the moon made by ice crystals; a paraselene.','mock moons?'),
('Pugilist','A boxer.','pugilists?'),('Scratch sheet','A racing sheet listing the day’s entries and the horses scratched.','scratch sheets?'),('Semaphore','A signaling apparatus with arms or flags.','semaphores?'),
('Stirrup cup','A last drink taken in the saddle before riding off.','stirrup cups?'),('Woof','The threads woven across the warp in a fabric.','woof')]
text=''.join(open(f'{S}book/{f}.txt',encoding='utf-8').read()+'\n' for f in ['b1','b2','b3','b4'])
P=re.split(r'^\s*CHAPTER (\d+)\s*$',text,flags=re.M)
chap={int(P[k]):re.sub(r'\s+',' ',re.sub(r'-\n\s*','-',P[k+1])).replace('’',"'").lower() for k in range(1,len(P),2)}
def chs(pt):
    rx=re.compile(r'(?<![a-z])(?:'+pt+r')(?![a-z])'); return [n for n in range(1,83) if rx.search(chap[n])]
have={w['word'].lower():w for w in dj['words']}
existing=[w['word'] for w in dj['words'] if 51 in w['chapters']]
rows=[]; skipped=[]
for w,df,pt in V:
    cc=chs(pt)
    if w.lower() in have or 51 not in cc: skipped.append((w,w.lower() in have,51 in cc)); continue
    dj['words'].append({'word':w,'definition':df,'chapters':cc,'defined_in':51,'pattern':pt}); rows.append((w,df))
print('skipped vocab:',skipped)
for w in existing: rows.append((w,re.sub(r' More in the <a .*?</a>\.','',have[w.lower()]['definition'])))
dj['vocab_chapters']=sorted(set(dj.get('vocab_chapters',[])+[51]))
rows.sort(key=lambda x:x[0].replace('’','').replace('é','e').lower())
assert '<h2>Vocabulary</h2>' not in h
h=h.rstrip('\n')+'\n<h2>Vocabulary</h2>\n<dl class="gloss">\n'+'\n'.join('<dt>%s</dt><dd>%s</dd>'%r for r in rows)+'\n</dl>\n'
h=curl_html(h)
h=re.sub(r'”([.,])',r'\1”',h)
tt=re.sub(r'<[^>]+>','',h); bad=[m for m in re.findall(r'.{30}["\'].{10}',tt)]
if bad: print('BAD',bad[:8])
for m in re.finditer(r'.{40} - .{40}',tt): print('DASH',m.group(0))
